    bottomLine:
      "The best fence stain for older, patched, or mismatched fencing where you want to hide the wood rather than showcase it. Paint-like durability with easier upkeep than actual paint.",
    description:
      "Solid color stain occupies a middle ground between paint and stain, and Behr Premium is the best execution of that concept we tested. Unlike transparent and semi-transparent stains that let wood grain show through, this is a heavily pigmented, opaque formula that behaves more like an exterior coating — it completely masks the wood underneath, which is exactly what you want on an older fence with patched boards, water stains, or mismatched replacement sections that would otherwise stick out under a see-through stain. We tested it on a 15-year-old pine fence that had been repaired in several spots with new boards, and after one coat, the entire fence looked uniform — no telltale color difference between old and new lumber. The water-based acrylic formula is genuinely low-odor and cleans up with soap and water, a meaningful convenience advantage over oil-based options, especially for indoor storage of brushes and rollers. UV resistance was excellent in our testing: the heavy pigment load blocks far more ultraviolet light than a transparent stain can, and after 12 months the color on our south-facing test panel showed only minor chalking, no visible fade. Because it forms more of a film than a true penetrating stain, application requires more attention to technique — back-brushing is necessary to avoid lap marks, and Behr recommends two coats for full coverage and maximum durability. Coverage is roughly 200-300 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth board fencing. The tradeoff with any solid stain is that it will eventually show wear at contact points like gate hinges, latch areas, and post tops where hands and hardware create friction, and once that happens, touch-up requires matching the exact tint rather than simply adding another coat of a natural wood tone. For fences you want to look freshly painted rather than naturally stained, and for older fencing that needs cosmetic camouflage, Behr Premium Solid is the strongest choice on the market.",
  },
  {
    id: "cabot-australian-timber-oil",
