      "The best fence stain for cedar and redwood fences where showcasing the wood's natural beauty is the priority. Nothing else in our test made premium wood grain pop like this did.",
    description:
      "Cabot Australian Timber Oil was developed specifically to enhance premium hardwoods, and on our cedar fence test section, the difference versus generic pine-formulated stains was obvious within minutes of application. The tung-oil-based formula soaks deep into cedar's naturally open grain, pulling out the reddish-amber undertones and the subtle color variation between heartwood and sapwood that a flatter, more opaque stain would simply cover up. Where Behr's solid stain is designed to hide wood character, Cabot's translucent tones are engineered to celebrate it, which makes it the clear pick for anyone who paid a premium for cedar or redwood fencing and wants that investment to show. In our water-beading test, Cabot outperformed every other oil-based stain we tried — droplets sat in tight beads on treated cedar boards for the entire 12-month test window, even through a wet spring with three weeks of near-daily rain. The tung oil formulation also includes UV-absorbing additives and a mildewcide package, both of which paid off in our shaded test section: a portion of the fence that stays damp under tree cover showed zero mold or algae growth after a full year, while an untreated control board in the same spot developed visible green staining within four months. Application is straightforward with a natural-bristle brush, pad applicator, or sprayer with back-brushing, and the oil penetrates quickly enough that a single coat is usually sufficient on tight-grained cedar, though rough-sawn or weathered wood benefits from two. Coverage runs about 100-150 square feet per gallon on cedar due to its higher absorption rate. Cabot recommends at least 48 hours of dry weather after application before the fence faces heavy rain, so timing around your local forecast matters more with this product than with faster-curing alternatives. Reapplication interval is 2-3 years depending on sun exposure. It costs noticeably more per gallon than Ready Seal or Olympic, but for cedar and redwood fencing specifically, the payoff in grain enhancement and water repellency justifies the premium.",
  },
