    cons: [
      "Shortest reapplication interval in our test — noticeable fade within 12-18 months",
      "Thinner formula provides less UV and water protection than every other product tested",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "An acceptable entry-level choice for small budgets or short-term projects, but our testing showed it needs reapplication sooner than every other stain on this list.",
    description:
      "Thompson's WaterSeal is likely the most recognized name in wood waterproofing thanks to decades of retail presence and marketing, so we included it as a baseline against which to measure the more specialized products in our lineup. It delivers exactly what the low price promises: basic waterproofing and light color in a fast, simple, one-coat application that's available at literally every hardware store in the country. On a fresh pressure-treated pine test section, initial water beading was respectable, and the fast dry time (often usable within 4-6 hours) makes it appealing for quick weekend projects, small gates, or short fence runs where you don't want to invest in a premium product. However, over our 12-month test period, Thompson's showed the fastest decline in performance of anything in the lineup. Water beading noticeably weakened by month 6, and by month 12, the treated boards were absorbing water at a rate much closer to untreated wood than any of the other six products tested. Color fade followed a similar pattern — the semi-transparent brown tone had visibly dulled and grayed by the one-year mark, well ahead of every competitor except for a slight edge over some low-end alternatives we didn't include in the final seven. This isn't necessarily a knock against Thompson's for its intended use case; it's formulated and priced as an economical, entry-level product rather than a long-term premium stain, and for homeowners staining a small section of fence, a single gate, or a rental property where minimizing upfront cost matters more than multi-year durability, it remains a reasonable choice. Coverage runs about 150-300 square feet per gallon depending on the specific formula variant. Just budget for reapplication within 12-18 months rather than the 2-3 year window that Ready Seal, Cabot, or SuperDeck can deliver, and understand that the tradeoff for the lowest price on this list is the shortest lifespan.",
