        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Fences Need Staining -->
        <div id="why-stain-fence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Fences Need Staining (And Why It Actually Works)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood fences face a harsher environment than almost any other exterior wood surface on your property. Unlike a deck that's partially shaded by the house or shielded by railings, a fence typically stands fully exposed on both sides, taking direct sun for most of the day and catching wind-driven rain from every angle. That constant exposure accelerates two damaging processes: <strong>UV degradation</strong> and <strong>moisture cycling</strong>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet light breaks down <strong>lignin</strong>, the natural polymer that binds wood fibers together and gives lumber its structural rigidity. As lignin breaks down at the surface, the wood turns a dull silvery-gray, becomes brittle, and starts to develop surface checking — those small splits running along the grain. This process is purely cosmetic at first, but over years it erodes the wood's surface fibers, leaving it rougher and more porous, which in turn makes it absorb even more water. That's where the second threat compounds the first: <strong>unprotected wood absorbs rainwater and snowmelt directly into its cell structure</strong>, causing the boards to swell. When the wood dries out, it shrinks back down. This constant swell-and-shrink cycle, repeated across hundreds of rain events per year, is what causes cupping, warping, and eventually the loosening of fasteners as the wood works itself around the nails and screws holding it together.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quality <strong>fence stain</strong> interrupts both processes. UV-blocking pigments and additives absorb or reflect ultraviolet light before it can degrade the lignin at the surface, which is why solid and semi-transparent stains with heavier pigment loads consistently outlast clear, unpigmented sealers in sun-exposure testing. Meanwhile, water-repellent resins and oils create a surface that sheds rainwater — causing it to bead and roll off — rather than absorbing it into the wood cells. This keeps the moisture content of the wood more stable through wet and dry cycles, which is the single biggest factor in how long a fence lasts before boards start to warp, split, or rot.
          </p>

          <p>
            It's also worth noting that different <strong>wood species</strong> respond differently to sun and moisture. Pressure-treated pine, the most common fence material, is relatively soft and porous, meaning it absorbs stain readily but also loses moisture and takes on water quickly if left unprotected. Cedar and redwood are naturally more rot- and insect-resistant thanks to their own internal oils and tannins, but they still fade to gray under UV exposure and benefit enormously from a stain that enhances rather than fights their natural coloring. This is why we tested across pressure-treated pine, cedar, and redwood sections rather than a single wood type — a stain's real-world performance depends heavily on what species it's going onto.
          </p>

