        <div id="types-of-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Fence Stain: Which One Is Right for You?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best fence stain</strong> for your project depends on how much of the natural wood grain you want visible, whether you're staining pine, cedar, or redwood, and whether you prioritize maximum durability or low-odor, easy cleanup. Here's how the main categories break down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transparent Stains</h3>
          <p>
            Transparent stains add almost no pigment, letting the wood's natural color and grain show through completely while adding a thin layer of water repellency and mild UV protection. They're the least durable category and require the most frequent reapplication — often annually in full sun — because there's simply not enough pigment to meaningfully block ultraviolet light. Best for: freshly installed cedar or redwood fencing where you want to preserve the exact natural look for as long as possible, accepting more frequent maintenance in exchange.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Semi-Transparent Stains</h3>
          <p>
            Semi-transparent stains, like <strong>Ready Seal</strong>, <strong>Cabot Australian Timber Oil</strong>, <strong>Sherwin-Williams SuperDeck</strong>, and <strong>Defy Extreme</strong>, add enough pigment to meaningfully block UV rays and add rich color while still letting the wood grain and texture show through. This is the most popular category for fence staining because it strikes the best balance between protection and natural appearance. Best for: most pressure-treated pine, cedar, and redwood fences where you want visible wood character along with strong, long-lasting protection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solid Color Stains</h3>
          <p>
            Solid color stains, like <strong>Behr Premium Solid Color Waterproofing Stain</strong>, are heavily pigmented and behave more like a thin exterior paint — they completely hide the wood grain and any surface imperfections underneath. They deliver the strongest UV blocking of any stain category because of their heavy pigment load, and they're the best choice for masking old, weathered, or patched fencing. Best for: older fences with mismatched boards or repairs, or homeowners who simply prefer a uniform, paint-like appearance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil-Based vs. Water-Based Formulas</h3>
          <p>
            Independent of pigment level, every stain is also either <strong>oil-based</strong> or <strong>water-based</strong>. Oil-based stains, like Ready Seal and Cabot, penetrate deeper into the wood grain and generally deliver the best water-beading longevity because the oil becomes part of the wood's cell structure rather than sitting on top of it. Water-based stains, like Defy Extreme, Olympic Maximum, and Behr Premium, are lower odor, clean up with soap and water, and don't amber or darken with age the way oil can — but they tend to sit closer to the surface and often need slightly more frequent reapplication for the same level of protection.
          </p>

